  "insights": [
   "Investment banks are increasingly using 'crash puts' (also known as cliquets or stability notes) to hedge tail risk associated with providing total return swaps to leveraged single-stock ETFs.",
   "These OTC derivatives allow banks to transfer 'gap risk'\u2014the risk of a single-session stock crash exceeding the ETF's net assets\u2014to institutional investors in exchange for high premiums.",
   "Premiums for these crash puts have surged due to immense demand; for example, BNP Paribas pitches for SK Hynix gap puts rose from 3.5% in March to 6.5% in May.",
   "The market for leveraged ETFs has grown significantly, with over 700 such products in the US, though total assets under management retreated from a June peak of $200B to $160B.",
   "Critics warn that the opacity and complexity of these products, combined with overlapping leverage and counterparty exposure, pose potential risks to financial stability."
